course: religion-ethics
lesson: 45
title: Whistle-blowing: when to tell
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Whistle-blowing means telling someone in charge about wrongdoing in a group you belong to, like a referee blowing a whistle to stop a foul.
explain: Picture a few classmates secretly picking on a younger child behind the bike sheds: everyone knows, nobody says anything, and the little one has no one on their side until someone is brave enough to tell.
explain: Telling to protect someone is not the same as "snitching" to get someone into trouble; protecting the vulnerable matters more than staying quiet for the group.
explain: Jesus taught that when someone does wrong you can first go to them on your own (Matthew 18:15), but when someone is in danger you go straight to a trusted adult; the Bible also says "speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves" (Proverbs 31:8).
explain: Speaking up can cost you friends, which is why it takes courage; for a child today, it means knowing your trusted adults and using your voice for people and animals who have none.
fact: Whistle-blowing means telling someone in charge about wrongdoing in a group you belong to.
fact: The name comes from a referee blowing a whistle to stop the game when there is a foul.
fact: When classmates secretly bully a younger child, protecting the vulnerable child matters more than loyalty to the group.
fact: Some children call any telling "snitching" to stop people reporting harm.
fact: Reporting harm to protect someone is different from telling just to get someone into trouble.
fact: If a friend has done damage, such as scratching a teacher's car, a good first step is to encourage them to own up.
fact: Responsibility, fairness to the victim and loyalty to the friend all matter in such a case.
fact: Whistle-blowers can lose friends, and adults can lose their jobs, so speaking up can be costly.
fact: Jesus taught going to the person first to put things right (Matthew 18:15).
fact: If someone is in danger or being hurt, you should go straight to a trusted adult rather than talking to the wrongdoer first.
fact: Trusted adults are safe grown-ups who can help, such as parents, teachers, youth leaders and school staff.
fact: Anonymous reporting means telling without giving your name; it can protect the reporter, but it can also be misused to spread false stories.
fact: Proverbs 31:8 says, "Speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves."
fact: Those without a voice include animals, babies and small children, and anyone too scared to speak.
exercise: What is whistle-blowing? | Telling someone in charge about wrongdoing in a group you belong to | Blowing a real whistle in class to make everyone stop talking at once | Telling lies about a group to get it into trouble | Keeping quiet about a problem to protect your friends
exercise: Why is it called "blowing the whistle"? | It is like a referee stopping the game when there is a foul | It comes from police officers who used to whistle songs | It comes from children whistling to warn friends of a teacher | It is because whistle-blowers always carry a real whistle
exercise: Some classmates are secretly bullying a younger child. What is the best-reasoned action? | Tell a trusted adult, because the younger child needs protecting more than the group needs secrecy | Stay quiet, because the group would be angry with you for telling | Join in a little, so the bullies do not turn on you next | Bully the bullies back, because they deserve to be hurt too
exercise: Why does protecting a vulnerable child outweigh group loyalty? | Because the child is being hurt and cannot stop it alone | Because groups never matter and friends are not important | Because telling will make you more popular with teachers | Because the younger child might give you a reward later
exercise: Someone calls you a snitch for reporting bullying. What is the best-reasoned answer? | Snitching is telling to get people in trouble; I told to stop someone being hurt | You're right, I'm a snitch, and I'm really sorry I ever said anything to anyone | You're the one who's a loser, so your opinion doesn't count | It's none of your business why I told, so go away
exercise: Why do some children call all telling "snitching"? | It can stop people from reporting harm that is going on | Because telling a teacher anything at all is always wrong, whatever it is | Because snitching is just a kind word for being helpful to grown-ups | Because the word comes from the Bible, where telling is forbidden
exercise: Your friend scratched a teacher's car. What is the best first step? | Encourage your friend to own up, because the teacher deserves to know | Say nothing, because loyalty to a friend matters far more than any car ever could | Tell everyone at school, so your friend is laughed at | Scratch it again so the friend is not the only one
exercise: In the case of the vandal friend, which things all matter? | Responsibility, fairness to the victim and loyalty to the friend | Only loyalty to the friend, because friends always come first, whatever they did | Only the cost of the car, because money is the only thing that really matters | Only whether anyone saw it, because that is what decides everything in the end
exercise: Why do people admire whistle-blowers? | Because doing right can cost them friends or jobs, so it takes courage | Because whistle-blowers always end up becoming rich and famous on the TV | Because whistle-blowing never has any cost at all | Because whistle-blowers are always right about everything
exercise: Is it fair to expect people to speak up when it costs them a lot? What is a well-reasoned answer? | We should still speak up about serious harm, but others should support and protect those who do | No, nobody should ever speak up if it costs them anything | Yes, and anyone who stays quiet is a coward who deserves punishment | No, because speaking up never changes anything anyway
exercise: What did Jesus teach about someone who has done wrong to you (Matthew 18:15)? | Go to them on your own first and try to put things right | Tell everyone what they did so that they feel properly ashamed | Never speak to them again for the rest of your life, whatever happens | Pretend nothing happened at all and keep it all to yourself
exercise: When should you go straight to an adult instead of talking to the person first? | When someone is in danger or being hurt | When the problem is small and easy to sort out | When the person is your good friend | When you are a bit annoyed with them
exercise: Who could be a trusted adult? | A parent, teacher, youth leader or school staff member | A stranger you meet online who says that they can help you | An older child who tells you to keep it all a big secret | Anyone at all you happen to meet, as long as they are an adult
exercise: What is anonymous reporting? | Telling about wrongdoing without giving your name | Telling about wrongdoing in front of the whole school | Telling a lie about someone to get them into trouble | Writing a letter to yourself about a problem
exercise: What is one good point and one bad point of anonymous reporting? | It can protect the reporter, but it can be misused for false stories | It is always perfectly safe and can never be misused | It always gets the reporter into big trouble, and nothing else ever happens | It is the only way to report anything at all
exercise: What does Proverbs 31:8 say? | Speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves | Keep quiet and let others sort out their own troubles | Speak only when you are spoken to by an adult | Never tell anyone about the wrong that others do
exercise: Who might have no voice in your school? | A younger child too scared to speak, or the class pet | The head teacher, who runs the whole school and its rules | The loudest children who shout all day in the playground | The parents who come along to watch the school play at Christmas
test: Your mate says, "Whistle-blowing is just being a grass, innit, it's the same thing." How do you put him right? | It's telling to stop real harm, not to get someone in trouble for fun | Yeah, it's the same, so nobody should ever do it | It only counts if you use a real whistle | It's the same thing, but it's fine as long as a teacher asks you to do it
test: Some Year 5s keep taking a little kid's lunch money behind the shed. Your friend says, "Not our problem, keep out of it." What is the best-reasoned reply? | It is our problem, he's being hurt and can't stop it, so let's tell a teacher | He's right, other people's problems are never any of our business, so keep out | Let's take the money off the Year 5s and keep it ourselves | Let's wait and see if it gets worse before we do anything
test: In the group chat: "whoever told Miss about the bullying is a dirty snitch, we'll find u." What is the best way to see this? | It's a threat meant to scare people off reporting harm, and it should be shown to an adult | It's a joke, so it can be ignored and nothing needs doing | It means whoever told did something really wrong | It's fair, because snitches deserve to be found out
test: Your best friend scratched Mr Hill's car with a key and says, "If you tell, we're done." What is the best-reasoned choice? | Urge your friend to own up first, and tell an adult if they won't, because Mr Hill deserves fairness | Keep quiet forever, because losing a friend is the worst thing | Tell Mr Hill it was someone else, so nobody you know is blamed | Report it anonymously and add a few extra lies to make it worse
test: Your brother says, "Jesus said always talk to the person first, so I'll just talk to the kid who's hitting little Amy." What is missing? | When someone is being hurt, go straight to a trusted adult, not just the person | Nothing, because talking to the person first is always enough, whatever happens | Jesus said never to talk to anyone about wrongdoing | He should hit the kid back first, then talk to him
test: A classmate who is not religious asks, "Why do Christians think they have to speak up for others?" What is the most accurate, respectful answer? | The Bible says to speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves, and many people of all beliefs agree | Because Christians think they are better than everyone else | Because Christians are not allowed to mind their own business | They don't, Christians are told to always keep quiet
test: Your sister says, "I'll tell Miss in a note without my name that Lily stole, even though I'm not sure." What is the problem? | Anonymous reports should be honest; accusing someone without being sure could hurt them unfairly | There is no problem, anonymous notes can say anything | The only problem is that Miss might find out who wrote it | Notes are never allowed, so she should shout it in class
test: Dad says, "In my old job a woman reported the boss for cheating customers and got sacked for it." What does this show? | Speaking up can be costly, which is why it takes courage and needs protecting | She was wrong to speak up, because she lost her job | Bosses are always right about everything, so she should have kept quiet and done her job | Speaking up always leads to a reward in the end
test: The neighbours leave their dog chained outside with no water in the heat. What is the best-reasoned response? | Tell your parents so they can help or call an animal charity; the dog can't speak for itself | Ignore it, because animals don't matter nearly as much as people, and it is their dog, not yours | Climb over the fence and take the dog home yourself | Throw stones at the neighbours' window to teach them a lesson
test: You're not sure who to tell. Which of these is a safe trusted adult? | Your class teacher or a youth leader you know at church | A friendly stranger you have been chatting to in an online game | A teenager who says "keep this just between the two of us" | Whoever happens to be standing nearest to you at the bus stop
test: A judge at the olympiad asks: "Isn't telling on classmates disloyal?" What is the best-reasoned answer? | Loyalty matters, but not to people who are harming someone weaker; protecting them comes first | Yes, telling is always disloyal, so it is always wrong | No, because loyalty to classmates never matters at all | It depends only on whether you like the people involved
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the act termed "whistle-blowing"? | The disclosure to a competent authority of wrongdoing within a body to which the discloser belongs | The circulation of false allegations with a view to injuring a rival | The concealment of wrongdoing in the interests of a group's reputation | The signalling of the commencement of a sporting fixture by an official
exam: Given that a member of a group is being harmed by other members, identify the course of action most consistent with sound ethical reasoning. | Disclosure to a trusted adult, the protection of the vulnerable outweighing loyalty to the group | Maintenance of silence, loyalty to the group being of paramount and overriding importance in every case | Retaliation against the perpetrators by equivalent means | Deferral of any action until such time as the harm has increased
exam: Select the response consistent with the teaching recorded in Matthew 18:15. | One who has been wronged ought first to approach the wrongdoer privately with a view to reconciliation | One who has been wronged ought first to denounce the wrongdoer publicly | One who has been wronged ought to sever all relations with the wrongdoer forthwith | One who has been wronged ought to conceal the matter from the wrongdoer indefinitely and from all others besides
exam: Identify the principal disadvantage of anonymous reporting. | The facility may be misused for the communication of false or malicious allegations | The facility invariably reveals the identity of the person reporting to those accused | The facility is available exclusively to persons in authority | The facility affords no protection whatsoever to the person reporting
